//! [find-package-only]
The command is defined in the \c Multimedia component of the \c Qt6 package, which
can be loaded like so:

\badcode
find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS Multimedia)
\endcode
//! [find-package-only]

// \1 is the component name passed to find_package, e.g. "Multimedia",
// "MultimediaWidgets", "SpatialAudio".
// \2 is the target_link_libraries keyword, e.g. "PUBLIC" or "PRIVATE".
// \3 is the CMake target to link against, e.g. "Qt6::Multimedia", "Qt::MultimediaWidgets".
//! [find-and-link]
\code
find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED COMPONENTS \1)
target_link_libraries(mytarget \2 \3)
\endcode
//! [find-and-link]

// \1 is the module display name, e.g. "Qt Multimedia", "Qt Spatial Audio".
// \2 is the QML import statement, e.g. "import QtMultimedia", "import QtQuick3D.SpatialAudio".
//! [qml-import-intro]
If you are new to \1, the QML types can be
\l{qtqml import syntax}{imported} into an application using the following
statement in your \c {.qml} file.

\qml
\2
\endqml
//! [qml-import-intro]
